Required attribution — miden-crypto's RPO-256 known-answer vectors
------------------------------------------------------------------

File: `src/upstream_vectors.zig`.

`miden_rpo256` — the 19 `Rpo256::hash_elements([0, 1, .., i])` digests — is
reproduced from the `EXPECTED` constant of `miden-crypto/src/hash/
algebraic_sponge/rescue/rpo/tests.rs` in github.com/0xMiden/crypto, taken at
commit 475092a, when that project was still named
github.com/0xPolygonMiden/crypto (the clone command and the upstream file's
SHA-256 are in the consuming file's own header, the extraction command sits
above the table). Same character as the Winterfell values above — numbers lifted out of
upstream's Rust literals by script and re-emitted as Zig array literals, no
Rust source of any kind present.

These 19 values are the reason this section exists, and they are the only ones
in it that are owed. They are not in the RPO report and they do not follow from
the published specification: they are the digests of miden-crypto's OWN sponge
framing, whose padding rule and state layout are that project's design
decisions — upstream changed that layout in a breaking release and every digest
changed with it (`SPEC.md` records the divergence; `vectors_test.zig` asserts
that this framing and the report's disagree on every input, as they must).
Published nowhere but in that project's test source and lifted out of it by
script, they are reproduced third-party data in root NOTICE §0's sense.

`miden_ark1` / `miden_ark2` sit in the same file and are NOT taken in any
load-bearing sense — the position `mds_row` holds in the Winterfell section
above, and for the same reason. All 168 values reproduce from the published
generator: `params.zig` derives them from SHAKE256 seeded with the
specification's own seed string, and `constants_test.zig` asserts it equals
these tables element by element. They are this module's own numbers, which
upstream's agree with; the copy here is the pin that proves it, not a source of
truth. `published_inv_alpha` is co-extracted from the same project's
`rpo/tests.rs` and is likewise computed here (extended Euclid). Neither would
oblige anything standing alone; both are covered by this notice because they
share the file with `miden_rpo256`.

