Provenance — everything else in this module
-------------------------------------------

Unaffected by the above, and stated here so the boundary is visible.

  - The RPO constants (`params.zig`) are DERIVED at build time by this
    repository's own implementation of algorithm 5 of eprint 2020/1143, and
    reproduce miden-crypto's published tables exactly, all 168 values. Nothing
    is carried into that file; the copies of upstream's tables that live in
    `upstream_vectors.zig`, as pins, are covered above.
  - The permutation, its parameters and the constant-derivation rule are public
    specification facts from the RPO report
    (github.com/ASDiscreteMathematics/rpo) and eprint 2020/1143 — a public
    specification is not a copyrightable work (merger doctrine; see
    CONVENTIONS.md §5).
  - Running an upstream implementation, or reading a digest it published, as a
    byte-exact test ORACLE is root NOTICE §0's black-box relationship and needs
    no attribution. That reading is what this module used to rest on; see the
    next paragraph for where it stops.

RULED ON 2026-08-14 — the report vectors need no attribution; the miden values
do. The two upstreams this file previously left open now have answers, and they
are different answers.

  - `spec_128` / `spec_160` — the 38 published test vectors, 19 per instance —
    were extracted from `report/report.tex` in
    github.com/ASDiscreteMathematics/rpo: the LaTeX source of the Rescue-Prime
    Optimized report (eprint 2022/1577), from its "Test Vectors" listings under
    "128 Bits Instance" and "160 Bits Instance". That is a published
    SPECIFICATION, not an implementation — root NOTICE §0's first bullet and
    CONVENTIONS.md §5 put spec material outside the attribution rule entirely:
    it is cited, not attributed, and the citation lives in `SPEC.md`. The
    extraction command above each table parses `report.tex` and the file header
    records that file's SHA-256, so this is checkable rather than asserted.
    Nothing is owed to the RPO authors for these values. Note the boundary: the
    same repository also holds `reference_implementation/
    rescue_prime_optimized.sage`, which IS an implementation — no table in
    `upstream_vectors.zig` comes from it (see the open item below).
  - `miden_rpo256` / `miden_ark1` / `miden_ark2` came out of miden-crypto's
    Rust source, an implementation, and are attributed above. The licence is
    now established and is not what the file's previous wording feared unknown:
    upstream offers MIT or Apache-2.0 and this repository elects MIT.

STILL OPEN, and stated rather than left implicit — a level below this file's
subject, but visible from it. `params.zig`'s own doc comment describes that
file as "a line-by-line port of `get_round_constants`, `get_alphas` and
`get_mds` from the authors' reference implementation
(`rescue_prime_optimized.sage`)". No numbers are carried from it — the
constants are generated and the two MDS rows are the report's own published
table (`params.zig` says so at the literal) — but "port" is the word that made
`minisign` acquire an attribution file this same day, and
github.com/ASDiscreteMathematics/rpo is licensed Apache-2.0, which unlike
miden-crypto offers no MIT alternative. Either the doc comment overstates what
was taken from the sage (an algorithm published in the report, re-expressed) or
an attribution is owed there. That is a question about `params.zig`, not about
the data files this notice covers, and nothing above depends on the outcome.

